1
00:00:01,550 --> 00:00:05,193
Your AI agent is a genius locked in
an empty room.

2
00:00:05,226 --> 00:00:06,864
It can't read your email.

3
00:00:06,897 --> 00:00:08,535
It can't touch your database.

4
00:00:08,568 --> 00:00:10,540
It can't even check the weather.

5
00:00:10,574 --> 00:00:14,551
Everything impressive you've ever watched it do
came through one small hole

6
00:00:14,584 --> 00:00:15,553
in the wall.

7
00:00:15,587 --> 00:00:19,564
In late twenty twenty-four, that hole got a
universal standard.It's called MCP.

8
00:00:19,597 --> 00:00:24,911
And by the end of this video, you'll know how
to plug your AI into almost

9
00:00:24,945 --> 00:00:25,246
anything.

10
00:00:36,279 --> 00:00:38,495
First, the mess it replaced.

11
00:00:38,540 --> 00:00:43,922
Say you have four AI apps, and five
tools you want them

12
00:00:43,967 --> 00:00:44,826
to use.

13
00:00:44,872 --> 00:00:45,279
Email.

14
00:00:45,324 --> 00:00:45,731
Slack.

15
00:00:45,776 --> 00:00:46,183
GitHub.

16
00:00:46,228 --> 00:00:47,088
A database.

17
00:00:47,133 --> 00:00:47,992
A browser.

18
00:00:48,037 --> 00:00:51,158
Before MCP, every connection was
custom code.

19
00:00:51,203 --> 00:00:53,419
Four apps times five tools.

20
00:00:53,464 --> 00:00:58,846
Twenty integrations, written by hand, each one
breaking in its own special

21
00:00:58,891 --> 00:00:59,298
way.

22
00:00:59,343 --> 00:01:04,273
Every new tool meant rebuilding the same
bridge, again and again.

23
00:01:09,518 --> 00:01:11,045
MCP kills that multiplication.

24
00:01:11,084 --> 00:01:12,611
It's one standard port.

25
00:01:12,650 --> 00:01:14,960
The tool builds one MCP server.

26
00:01:14,999 --> 00:01:17,701
The AI app builds one MCP client.

27
00:01:17,740 --> 00:01:20,441
And now anything can talk to anything.

28
00:01:20,480 --> 00:01:23,573
Four plus five instead of four times five.

29
00:01:23,612 --> 00:01:28,663
People call it the USB port for AI, and
for once, the nickname

30
00:01:28,702 --> 00:01:29,446
is accurate.

31
00:01:34,685 --> 00:01:38,093
Here's the actual architecture, and it's
simpler than it sounds.

32
00:01:38,128 --> 00:01:41,536
Your AI app, like Claude Code, is
called the host.

33
00:01:41,570 --> 00:01:46,011
For every outside system it wants to reach,
the host opens a client.

34
00:01:46,046 --> 00:01:49,109
Each client holds one connection to
one MCP server.

35
00:01:49,144 --> 00:01:54,273
And the server is just a small program sitting
in front of a real system,

36
00:01:54,308 --> 00:01:54,618
translating.

37
00:01:57,852 --> 00:01:59,841
And this is the beautiful part.

38
00:01:59,875 --> 00:02:03,212
When the agent connects, the server
hands over a menu.

39
00:02:03,246 --> 00:02:04,560
Here are my tools.

40
00:02:04,594 --> 00:02:06,246
Here's what each one does.

41
00:02:06,280 --> 00:02:07,931
Here's what each one needs.

42
00:02:07,965 --> 00:02:10,291
The agent reads that menu at runtime.

43
00:02:10,325 --> 00:02:15,348
Which means you can hand your AI a brand new
ability, and nobody rewrites the

44
00:02:15,381 --> 00:02:15,685
app.

45
00:02:15,718 --> 00:02:17,707
You just plug in another server.

46
00:02:22,941 --> 00:02:25,483
Watch one real call, end to end.

47
00:02:25,520 --> 00:02:28,430
You ask your agent to check open issues.

48
00:02:28,467 --> 00:02:31,745
The model picks the right tool
from the menu.

49
00:02:31,782 --> 00:02:34,324
The client wraps it in a message.

50
00:02:34,361 --> 00:02:36,903
The server calls the actual GitHub API.

51
00:02:36,940 --> 00:02:41,324
And the result flows all the way back into
the model's context.

52
00:02:41,360 --> 00:02:45,744
To you, it just looks like your AI can
suddenly see GitHub.

53
00:02:45,781 --> 00:02:49,428
Under the hood, it's this exact relay,
every single time.

54
00:02:52,665 --> 00:02:58,015
And because the port is standard, an entire
ecosystem exploded around it.

55
00:02:58,060 --> 00:02:59,364
Thousands of servers.

56
00:02:59,409 --> 00:02:59,814
Databases.

57
00:02:59,859 --> 00:03:00,263
Browsers.

58
00:03:00,308 --> 00:03:01,163
Payment systems.

59
00:03:01,208 --> 00:03:02,062
Design tools.

60
00:03:02,107 --> 00:03:05,659
Even other AI companies adopted the
same standard.

61
00:03:05,704 --> 00:03:11,054
Build one server for your product, and
every AI app on earth

62
00:03:11,099 --> 00:03:12,853
can suddenly use it.

63
00:03:12,898 --> 00:03:14,651
That's why MCP won.

64
00:03:19,896 --> 00:03:21,999
But one warning, and it's serious.

65
00:03:22,034 --> 00:03:24,137
That menu the server hands over?

66
00:03:24,173 --> 00:03:25,563
The model trusts it.

67
00:03:25,598 --> 00:03:29,483
A malicious server can hide instructions inside
tool descriptions, poison your

68
00:03:29,519 --> 00:03:33,047
agent's behavior, or quietly walk your
data out the door.

69
00:03:33,082 --> 00:03:35,542
So treat MCP servers like browser
extensions.

70
00:03:35,577 --> 00:03:37,680
Only plug in what you trust.

71
00:03:37,716 --> 00:03:43,026
Never point your agent at a random server just
because a list on the internet

72
00:03:43,061 --> 00:03:44,451
said it was cool.

73
00:03:47,487 --> 00:03:48,189
One port.

74
00:03:48,226 --> 00:03:49,668
A menu of tools.

75
00:03:49,705 --> 00:03:50,777
Plug in anything.

76
00:03:50,814 --> 00:03:55,952
That's MCP, and now you understand the wiring
behind every AI agent demo you've

77
00:03:55,989 --> 00:03:56,692
ever seen.

78
00:03:56,729 --> 00:03:58,910
Subagents gave your AI more brains.

79
00:03:58,946 --> 00:04:01,867
MCP gave it hands on the real world.

80
00:04:01,904 --> 00:04:07,042
Next time, we go further: we teach an agent
brand new skills it wasn't

81
00:04:07,079 --> 00:04:07,781
born with.

82
00:04:07,818 --> 00:04:10,369
Subscribe to codewired, and get wired in.
